.TH KEPLERS_HORIZON 1 "2025-12-31" "Version 1.0" "Kepler's Horizon Command Reference"
.SH NAME
.B kh \fR "\- two-player tactical space combat game command interface"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kh
.br
.B kh \-\^\-help
.br
.B kh \-\^\-version
.br
.B kh \-\^\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
.br
.B kh \-\^\-dbusr "\fI USER\fR"
.br
.B "    " \-\^\-dbpass " \fIPASSWORD\fR"
.br
.B "    " \-\^\-dbname " \fIDATABASE\fR"
.br
.B "    " \-\^\-dbhost " \fIHOST\fR"
.br
.B kh \-\^\-monitor
.br
.B kh \-\^\-log " \fIFILE\fR"
.br

.SH DESCRIPTION
Kepler's Horizon is a two-player tactical game of space combat where players build individualized ships, move them across a hex-based star map, and resolve combat without dice. The objective is to occupy opponent base star hexes and accumulate victory points.

This manual describes all available commands in the Kepler's Horizon command language as defined by the game grammar.

.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Generic Program Information"
.TP
.B --help
Output a usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\^\-version
Output the version number of
.B kh
and exit.
.SS "Database Connection"
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\^\-dbhost " HOST"
The hostname to reach for the database. Default is
.B 127.0.0.1
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\^\-dbusr " USER"
Access the underlying database as \fIUSER\fR
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\^\-dbname  " DB_NAME"
The name of the underlying database.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\^\-dbpass " PASSWORD"
The password for \fIUSER\fR.
.SS "Database Connection"
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\^\-port " PORT"
The port number the server listens to for requests via REST.  The default is 8080.
.SS Logging
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\^\-log " FILE"
Logging from the server written to \fIFILE\fR.
.SS Monitor
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\^\-monitor
Put the server into monitor mode. The server will accept commands
from terminal prompt.
.SH GAME OVERVIEW
.SS Map Components
.TP
.B StarHex
A map hex containing a large or small star (graph node)
.TP
.B BaseStarHex
A map hex containing a large star (6 total: 3 per player)
.TP
.B SpaceHex
A map hex without a star or warpline
.TP
.B WarpLine
Bi-directional edges connecting stars for instantaneous travel

.SS Ship Types
.TP
.B WarpShip (W#)
Ships capable of interstellar travel via warplines
.TP
.B SystemShip (S##)
Ships that cannot travel between stars unless carried by WarpShips

.SH GAME SEQUENCE
Each player-turn consists of the following phases in order:
.PP
.RS
1. Count Victory Points
.br
2. Build Ships (receive BP at start of each turn)
.br
3. Movement
.br
4. Resolve Combat
.br
5. SystemShip Pick and Drop
.br
6. End of Turn
.RE

.SH CONCEPTS
.SS Starting a Game
Games are started through the web interface, not via command line:
.PP
.RS
.B 1. Portal
.br
Login or create account at the web portal.
.PP
.B 2. Lobby
.br
View open games or create a new one.
.PP
.B 3. Join Game
.br
Join a game and take a seat (A or B). When both seats are filled, either player can start the game.
.PP
.B 4. Module Selection
.br
The game host selects the universe module:
.RS
.IP "\fBKepler's Horizon\fR"
The original frontier universe - humanity's expansion into the stars. Contains 28 star systems across Alpha and Beta territories.
.IP "\fB(Additional Modules)\fR"
Custom modules can be created with unique star maps, populations, species, and resources. See the wiki for the Module Authoring guide.
.RE
.RE

.SH MODULES
A \fBModule\fR defines a complete game universe. All milieu data (star systems, planets, species, populations, resources) is keyed by module_id.

The game engine is module-agnostic; only content varies between modules. Players select a module when creating a game.

See the wiki for the Module Authoring guide.

.SH COMMANDS
Commands are case-insensitive. Aliases are shown after commas.

.SS Turn Management Commands
.TP
.B next, n
Advance the active player to the next phase.
.RS
.PP
\fBSyntax:\fR next
.PP
\fBExample:\fR
.nf
> next
Phase advanced to: Movement
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B done, ZZ
Complete current player's turn and advance to opponent's first phase.
.RS
.PP
\fBSyntax:\fR done
.PP
\fBExample:\fR
.nf
> done
COMMAND: Your turn has ended. Standing down.
.fi
.RE

.SS Information Commands
.TP
.B status
Show game status and important records (current phase, turn number, BP, VP).
.RS
.PP
\fBSyntax:\fR status
.RE
.TP
.B fleet
Show manifest of your fleet (all ships and their current attributes).
.RS
.PP
\fBSyntax:\fR fleet
.PP
\fBExample:\fR
.nf
> fleet
Your Fleet (Player A):
  W1 The Vanguard  [PD:5 B:3 S:2 T:1 M:6] at ARVEN
  W2 The Sentinel  [PD:4 B:2 S:3 T:0 M:0] at BELIX
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B crt
Display the Combat Results Table
.TP
.B hex \fIlocation\fR
Show location and spatial information about the specified hex
.TP
.B online
Show which players are online and their connection times
.TP
.B stats
Display statistics about the game, your fleet, and opponent
.TP
.B fleet
Show manifest of your fleet (all ships and their current attributes)
.TP
.B system \fIname\fR
Show environmental information about the specified star system
.TP
.B galaxy
Complete overview of all systems and all ships in the game
.TP
.B score
Show current game status: whose turn, phase, credits, tech level, and victory points

.SS Economy Commands
.TP
.B extract scan
Scan current system for harvestable resources. Requires ship with LRS (Long Range Scanner).
.TP
.B extract \fIship\fR \fIresource\fR
Extract resource from current system into ship's cargo hold. Requires TB (Transporter Beam) or DR (Drones).
.RS
.PP
\fBResources:\fR FERROUS, RARE_EARTH, RADIOACTIVE, CRYSTALLINE, VOLATILE, WATER, ORGANIC, EXOTIC
.RE
.TP
.B market
Display current market prices with trend indicators (▲ rising, ─ stable, ▼ falling).
.RS
.PP
Prices fluctuate each round based on supply/demand and market forces.
.RE
.TP
.B market \fIresource\fR
Show price history for the specified resource over recent turns.
.TP
.B trade list
Show base trade prices for all resources.
.TP
.B trade buy \fIresource\fR \fIquantity\fR
Purchase resources at current market price, deducting credits.
.TP
.B trade sell \fIresource\fR \fIquantity\fR
Sell resources from cargo at 75% of market price.
.TP
.B trade transfer \fIfrom_ship\fR \fIto_ship\fR \fIresource\fR \fIquantity\fR
Move resources between ships at the same location.
.TP
.B fabricate list
Show available manufacturing recipes and their costs.
.TP
.B fabricate \fIrecipe\fR \fIquantity\fR
Manufacture items from raw materials in cargo. Recipes include:
.RS
.IP \fBmissiles\fR
Basic missiles (4 per batch). Cost: 2 Fe, 1 Rd, 1 Vo. Time: 1 round.
.IP \fBtubes\fR
Tube upgrade (+1 capacity). Cost: 5 Fe, 3 Re, 2 Cr. Time: 3 rounds.
.IP \fBbeams\fR
Beam upgrade (+1 rating). Cost: 8 Fe, 4 Re, 3 Cr. Time: 3 rounds.
.IP \fBscreens\fR
Screen upgrade (+1 rating). Cost: 6 Fe, 2 Re, 4 Cr. Time: 3 rounds.
.IP \fBtech\fR
Tech research (+1 level). Cost: 10 Re, 5 Cr, 2 Ex. Time: 5 rounds.
.RE
.TP
.B salvage \fIship\fR
Salvage wreckage at anomaly sites like The Graveyard (SYDRA). Requires Drones (DR).
Random outcome: 90% chance of recovering 2-5 units of random resources, 10% chance of hazard encounter dealing hull damage.

.SS Build Commands
.TP
.B build, b
Show current build state (active drafts, available BP)
.TP
.B build new \fIcode\fR \fIname\fR
.B bn \fIcode\fR \fIname\fR
Create a new ship draft with the specified code (e.g., W1, S01) and name
.TP
.B build drafts
.B bd
List all pending build drafts
.TP
.B build drafts \fIship_code\fR
.B bd \fIship_code\fR
Show detailed attributes of a specific draft ship
.TP
.B build set \fIattributes\fR,  \fBbs\fR \fIattributes\fR
Set attributes on the current draft ship. Attributes are specified as:
.RS
.TP
.B pd=\fIN\fR or d=\fIN\fR
Power/Drive strength (1 BP per unit, provides movement and combat power)
.TP
.B b=\fIN\fR
Beam weapon strength (1 BP per unit, max power for beam projector)
.TP
.B s=\fIN\fR
Screen strength (1 BP per unit, absorbs hits equal to power + tech level)
.TP
.B t=\fIN\fR
Number of missile tubes (1 BP per tube, requires 1 PD to fire)
.TP
.B m=\fIN\fR
Number of missiles (1 BP per 3 missiles, each does 2 + tech level hits)
.TP
.B sr=\fIN\fR
SystemShip racks (1 BP per rack, WarpShips only, carries 1 SystemShip per rack)
.PP
Example: build set pd=7 b=3 s=2 t=1 m=6
.br
Note: Warp Generator costs 5 BP (automatically added for WarpShips)
.RE
.TP
.B build commit \fIship_code\fR, \fBbc\fR \fIship_code\fR
Finalize and add draft ship to fleet (deducts BP)
.TP
.B build cancel \fIship_code\fR, \fBbx\fR \fIship_code\fR
Cancel draft ship (no BP deducted)

.SS Deployment Commands
.TP
.B deploy \fIship_code\fR \fIlocation\fR
Deploy newly built ship to specified base star hex
.RS
.PP
First deployment by either player claims that side of the map (3 base stars).
Subsequent deployments must be to the same side.
.RE

.SS Movement Commands
.TP
.B move \fIship_code\fR \fIdestination\fR [\fIvia...\fR]
.B m \fIship_code\fR \fIdestination\fR [\fIvia...\fR]
Move WarpShip along path to destination
.RS
.PP
\fBDestination:\fR Star system name or hex ID
.PP
\fBVia:\fR Optional intermediate waypoints
.PP
Movement costs 1 PD per hex or per warpline jump. Ships must stop at star hexes occupied by enemy ships (combat triggered).
.RE
.TP
.B pick \fIwarpship\fR \fIsystemship\fR
Load SystemShip onto WarpShip (costs 1 PD during movement)
.TP
.B drop \fIwarpship\fR \fIsystemship\fR
Unload SystemShip from WarpShip (costs 1 PD during movement)

.SS Combat Commands
.TP
.B combat, c
Show active combat status at all contested hexes
.TP
.B combat order \fIship\fR \fItactic\fR \fItarget\fR \fIallocation\fR
.B co \fIship\fR \fItactic\fR \fItarget\fR \fIallocation\fR
Issue combat order for one of your ships
.RS
.PP
\fBTactics:\fR attack (a), dodge (d), retreat (r)
.PP
\fBTarget:\fR Enemy ship code
.PP
\fBAllocation:\fR Power distribution (e.g., pd=5 b=3 s=2)
.PP
\fBExample:\fR co W3 attack S25 pd=5 b=3 s=2
.PP
For missiles, specify tube count and drive settings:
.br
co S25 dodge W3 pd=5 t=1 m=3
.br
(missile drive setting: 3)
.RE
.TP
.B combat commit, cc
Finalize all combat orders (locks them in)
.TP
.B combat cancel, cx
Cancel all pending combat orders
.TP
.B combat drafts, cd
Show your pending combat orders
.TP
.B combat apply \fIship\fR \fIdamage_allocation\fR
.B ca \fIship\fR \fIdamage_allocation\fR
Assign damage to ship attributes after combat resolution
.RS
.PP
\fBExample:\fR ca W3 pd=2 b=1 s=2
.PP
Distributes 5 total damage: 2 to PD, 1 to Beam, 2 to Screen
.RE

.SS Repair Commands
.TP
.B repair \fIship_code\fR \fIattributes\fR
Repair damaged ship at base star hex (1 BP per attribute point)
.RS
.PP
\fBExample:\fR repair W3 pd=2 b=1
.PP
Costs 3 BP total. Ship must start turn at your base hex.
.RE
.TP
.B resupply \fIship_code\fR m=\fIN\fR
Resupply missiles (1 BP per 3 missiles, up to original capacity)

.SH COMBAT RESOLUTION
.SS Combat Results Table
Outcomes determined by comparing drive allocations and tactics:
.PP
.B Drive Difference = Attacker Drive - Defender Drive
.PP
Results: Miss, Hit, Hit+1, Hit+2, or Escapes (for Retreat tactic)

.SS Power Allocation
Each combat order specifies how PD is allocated:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDrive:\fR Maneuverability (affects CRT outcomes)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBeam:\fR Weapon power (up to ship's beam maximum)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBScreen:\fR Defense (absorbs hits = power + tech level)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTubes:\fR Number of missiles to fire (costs 1 PD per tube)
.PP
Constraint: Drive + Beam + Screen + Tubes ≤ Current PD

.SS Damage Application
.IP 1. 3
Calculate hits from all weapons that hit
.IP 2. 3
Subtract Screen power + tech level (if Screen powered)
.IP 3. 3
Apply remaining "effective hits" to ship attributes (owner chooses where)
.IP 4. 3
Ship destroyed when all attributes except Warp Generator reduced to 0

.SS Special Combat Rules
.IP \(bu 2
Beams and Screens can be used together
.IP \(bu 2
Missiles cannot be used same round as Beams/Screens
.IP \(bu 2
Missiles always use Attack tactic with their own drive setting
.IP \(bu 2
SystemShips cannot Retreat (must be carried by WarpShip)
.IP \(bu 2
Combat ends when: all ships of one side destroyed/retreated, or 3 consecutive rounds with no unabsorbed damage (attacker must withdraw)

.SH MOVEMENT RULES
.IP 1. 3
WarpShips must stop at star hexes occupied by enemy ships
.IP 2. 3
WarpShips may move through space hexes with enemy ships
.IP 3. 3
WarpLines are treated as space hexes for movement
.IP 4. 3
Ships with PD=0 cannot move
.IP 5. 3
PD used for movement is not expended (full PD available for combat)
.IP 6. 3
Cannot move onto enemy base star hex during turn 1

.SH VICTORY CONDITIONS
.TP
.B Dominance Victory
Accumulate 3 victory points by occupying enemy base stars at start of your turn
.TP
.B Elimination Victory
Destroy all enemy ships
.TP
.B Draw
Neither player has effective ships remaining

